# Copy to .env.local and fill in. See the "Configuration" section of README.md
# for what each variable does and what happens when it is missing.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required in production. Optional locally (defaults to http://localhost:3001).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Absolute public origin, no trailing slash. Backs every canonical URL, Open
# Graph url, JSON-LD @id and sitemap.xml entry.
#
# On Vercel this falls back to VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL when unset, so it
# is usually only needed for a custom domain. A production build that resolves
# to localhost fails on purpose — and note that a localhost value set here is
# also picked up by `next build`, so override it for production builds.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3001

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional. Server-only — do not prefix any of these with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Raises the GitHub API limit from 60 requests/hour (unauthenticated) for the
# repo stats overlaid onto data/projects.ts. Only public-repo read scope is
# needed. Without it the site still renders in full, just without live stars,
# language and last-updated dates once the limit is hit.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Enables real email delivery from the contact form. Without it the form still
# validates and rate-limits, but sends nothing and falls back to a prefilled
# mailto: link.
# RESEND_API_KEY=

# Sender identity for contact-form email, e.g. "Your Name <hello@example.com>".
# Defaults to Resend's shared onboarding@resend.dev sender, which needs no
# domain setup but only delivers to the Resend account owner's own address.
# Set this once you have a verified domain.
# CONTACT_FROM_EMAIL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Admin console (/admin). Server-only — never prefix any of these with
# NEXT_PUBLIC_. See docs/admin-plan.md §4.
#
# When ADMIN_USERNAME, ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH or ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET is unset,
# /admin returns 404 — not a login page. A console with no configured password
# should not exist as a reachable surface, and a preview deploy that lacks the
# secrets should not advertise one.
#
# Generate all three at once, on your own machine:
#     node scripts/hash-password.mjs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The single operator's username. Compared in constant time, so the form
# cannot be used to test whether a username exists.
# ADMIN_USERNAME=

# scrypt.N.r.p.salt.hash, produced by the script above. The plaintext password
# never exists in the repo, in Vercel, or in a log line — only this string.
#
# The separator is a dot and the salt and digest are base64url, so the value
# pastes in exactly as printed — no quotes, no escaping, here or in Vercel.
# That is deliberate rather than cosmetic: Next's env loader expands $NAME
# inside this file, and the earlier $-separated format arrived at the app as
# "scrypt6384", failing every login with the same generic message a wrong
# password gives. Variables set in the Vercel dashboard were never affected,
# so the symptom only ever appeared locally.
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=

# 32 random bytes, base64. Signs the session cookie (HMAC-SHA256). Rotating it
# logs every session out everywhere — that is the revoke-all button.
# ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=

# Session generation. Any positive integer; unset means 1. Raising it rejects
# every cookie signed under the previous number, so it is the "sign out
# everywhere" button — immediate, and without regenerating the signing secret
# (which is the other way, and needs a redeploy). Bump it if a laptop goes
# missing or a session was left open somewhere you no longer control.
# ADMIN_SESSION_EPOCH=1

# Optional second factor at login. Base32, from `node scripts/hash-password.mjs`,
# which also prints an otpauth:// URI to scan. When set, the login form asks for
# a 6-digit code; when unset there is no second factor and no field. Turning it
# off again is deleting this variable — there is no enrolment state to unwind.
#
# What it actually protects against: a password that leaked on its own, through
# a reused credential or a shared machine. It does NOT help if the whole
# environment leaks, because the secret is in it — that is what the token's
# repository scope and expiry are for.
# ADMIN_TOTP_SECRET=

# A SECOND, SEPARATE GitHub token — not GITHUB_TOKEN above, which is read-only
# and used on the public render path. This one writes commits, so it should be
# a fine-grained PAT scoped to this repository only, with Contents: Read and
# write and nothing else, and an expiry date set. Without it the console loads
# and refuses every save.
# GITHUB_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployment status and traffic (/admin). Both optional, both read-only.
# Without them the console works exactly as before: a save says "Vercel is
# building" and never learns whether the build succeeded, and the dashboard
# omits its traffic panel.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A Vercel API token with read access. Used to report whether the build a save
# triggered actually succeeded — which is the one thing the commit link cannot
# say, and the reason a broken build used to look identical to a working one.
# VERCEL_TOKEN=

# The project's id, from its Vercel settings page. Vercel injects VERCEL_URL and
# VERCEL_ENV at runtime but NOT this one, so it has to be set by hand.
# VERCEL_PROJECT_ID=

# Only needed when the project belongs to a team rather than a personal account.
# VERCEL_TEAM_ID=
